00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.480
about focusing on
things that allow

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:15.510
you to do things differently,
differentiate yourselves.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:16.970
Now I think it's
really important

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:20.610
when we talk about tape
and long term storage

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.740
that we call out this classic
difference between backup

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:24.700
and archive.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.230
A lot of people
conflate the two.

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:32.130
Backup really is about recovery.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:35.740
It is a really
important distinction.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.580
It's a second or third copy,
while archived is really

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:41.290
for legal or
regulatory requirements

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:44.740
usually to meet
an important SLA.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:46.870
It's also the case that
some people turn backups

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:52.154
into archives which is a
cause of significant, really

00:05:52.154 --> 00:05:53.278
significant inefficiencies.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.300
So that is sort of a
background or a precursor.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.258
Let's get into the heart
of this conversation--

00:06:00.258 --> 00:06:02.800
the capital and
operational cost associated

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.580
with both tape and cloud.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.980
So at a high level
disk, tape, and cloud--

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.600
so I've included
disk here-- if you're

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.680
looking at the cost
of long term storage,

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.510
this is a very just get
high level snapshot.

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:16.990
Capital outlay, the
winners are here in green.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:20.460
Capital outlay cloud wins
because there's virtually

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.700
no upfront capital outlay.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:25.360
Capacity costs, usually
tape wins when you just

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.254
look at the cost per
terabyte or per gigabyte

00:06:29.254 --> 00:06:30.170
over a period of time.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:33.390
From a capacity standpoint,
it's usually tape that wins.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:35.840
Shipping and logistics, cloud
wins because there are none.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.340
You don't ship it.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:36.690
You don't ship disk.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:37.820
You don't ship tape.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.430
I mean you don't ship cloud
like you ship a disk or a tape.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:42.590
Networking cost, tape wins.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:45.420
There's none or
very low, in fact.

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:49.430
Personnel cost is lowest
with cloud or disk

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:52.340
because they're more automated,
more direct processes.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:55.300
And recovery costs are low
with cloud and disk, as well.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:58.594
But what I really want to focus
on here are tape and cloud.

00:06:58.594 --> 00:07:00.010
There's been a lot
of conversation

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.340
on disk versus tape
and now I really

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.490
want to talk about
cloud and tape.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:10.930
So let's first go deep on the
capital upfront cost of tape.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.760
Now if you use tape
today, hopefully this

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:14.810
will look familiar to you.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.420
The biggest upfront cost
is buying your library,

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:18.930
buying your autoloader.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:23.131
This can range from 3k to 50k or
into the hundreds of thousands

00:07:23.131 --> 00:07:23.630
of dollars.

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:25.550
It could be a little
bit lower-- I've

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:28.030
purchased an autoloader
that cost less than that--

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.030
but it also could
be a lot higher.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:33.440
I've seen libraries that run
into the hundreds of thousands

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:34.580
of dollars.

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:37.650
It's typically capitalized
over three years,

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:42.170
but the lifespan typically
is longer than that.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:45.290
The next thing that people think
about in terms upfront cost

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:46.100
is tapes.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:50.390
You need to buy tapes up
front both for your backup

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:51.520
and archival.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.690
And this can significantly
vary depending

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.700
on what your backup
schedules are,

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:02.990
but generally, you might pay
somewhere between $30 and $150.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:05.140
I think the street
price for an LTO-6

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:09.500
is around $30 and the LTO-7s
up around $130, $140 right now.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:11.620
And they're rated for
long periods of time,

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:15.030
but nobody or very few people
or very few sophisticated people

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:21.156
that I know actually trust
those long periods of time.

00:08:21.156 --> 00:08:22.530
You may need to
make improvements

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:28.420
to your server and your
networking or your SAN.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:32.010
You may have to make changes
to your server and maybe buy

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.200
a fibre channel card,
things like that,

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:42.150
in order to backup or move
your data to a tape device.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:44.810
And then, of course,
these are physical devices

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:46.150
that need to be shipped.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:51.590
They need to be unpacked and
plugged in and put in a rack.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:55.500
Then they need to be
physically managed

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.010
up front so these are the costs.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.260
You have to buy the loader,
you have to buy the tapes,

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:03.259
you have to make changes to
your server, your networking,

00:09:03.259 --> 00:09:05.550
and then you have to actually
spend some money up front

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.200
to get the thing started up.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.080
Generally high cost.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.130
Now let's move
from upfront cost.

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:16.140
I should say for cloud, I've
originally had made a slide

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.240
and said, these are the
capital costs upfront.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.240
Really, are no capital
costs up front for cloud.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.970
The capital cost
up front for cloud

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.980
are really just a
learning curve it takes

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:28.980
and it's very, very small
learning curve, low learning

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.090
curve.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:34.620
So let's move from
capital cost up front cost

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:36.520
to operational cost.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:37.020
Right?

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:41.130
Or these are your ongoing
expenses, in fact.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:42.860
Let's start with cloud.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:48.765
And these are the six
things that typically you

00:09:48.765 --> 00:09:50.140
should take into
account when you

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:53.830
think about your ongoing
cost of storing things

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:54.790
long term in a cloud.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:10:00.470
First, ingestion or getting
the data to the cloud.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:02.814
This is not trivial.

00:10:02.814 --> 00:10:04.480
If you have significant
amounts of data,

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.850
you can do it either
online directly

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:12.060
if you have good networks or
you can even do it offline.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:18.750
You can ship drives and have
them ingested by third parties.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:19.425
Storage.

00:10:19.425 --> 00:10:21.550
Storage is typically what
people focus on the most,

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:22.500
and rightfully so.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.390
It's by far, the largest
portion of your cost,

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.750
but remember storage
is all inclusive.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:31.450
It gives you the high level
of 11 nines of durability.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:34.156
I'll talk a little bit about
in a moment with online access

00:10:34.156 --> 00:10:35.780
and on demand, and
all that good stuff,

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:39.560
but that is typically
the highest cost.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:43.260
And it will range anywhere
from, at least with Google

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.290
and with other cloud
providers, this

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:48.910
is the general range from
2.6 cents down to a penny

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:50.970
a gigabyte.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:52.480
Operations.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:57.980
So this is our charges for
operations in the cloud.

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:00.250
This typically not an issue
for long term storage,

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:02.090
but I did actually
have a customer want

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:05.020
to have 800 million
files who wanted

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:07.990
to be kept as separate
objects and it cost him-- we

00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:11.700
charge a penny a gig, sorry, a
penny per 10,000 operations--

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:22.890
so it cost him $800 to do the
puts on his operations cost.

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:23.690
Excess reads.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:27.750
This is something that you
do need to think about.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:32.520
For long term storage, our tier
that I'll talk about a minute

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:35.640
is Nearline and that's
intended for long term storage.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:38.110
So it has a lower cost.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:41.530
It's less than 50%,
closer to about 30%,

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:44.960
the cost of traditional
online object storage,

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:50.750
but it also has a cost to
read the data because it's not

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:53.000
intended to be read frequently.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:58.510
And it's a cost of a
penny a gig for the reads.

00:11:58.510 --> 00:11:59.110
Networking.

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:00.990
There is an excess
burden on networking,

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:04.170
but this is really tricky
because network configurations

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.994
and costs associated with them
can vary so significantly.

00:12:06.994 --> 00:12:08.660
I think it's just
important to point out

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:11.540
that moving data from on-prem
to a private data center

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.680
or private data center to the
cloud for long term storage

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:19.050
does have some costs through
an increased network traffic.

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.730
And then finally, we are
talking about operational cost

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:23.730
of cloud, it's personnel.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.190
And you're going to
find, as I mentioned

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:28.870
early on, one of the
overriding themes

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.860
is that cloud and
disk, for that matter,

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:34.009
give you the ability
to optimize your data,

00:12:34.009 --> 00:12:35.550
to optimize your
entire corpus so you

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.600
can delete redundant
data, eliminate garbage.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.830
And this benefit, in fact, will
require some additional effort.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:44.200
So if you think about the
operational cost in cloud,

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:46.490
which is what we're
talking about on the slide,

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:50.350
you should, to be fair,
take that into account.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.830
And there will be some effort,
but of course that effort

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:57.730
will payoff many times
over as you reduce

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:00.190
the amount of data you have.

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:03.970
Now let's move on to the
operational cost of tape.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:05.870
And I've got two slides on this.

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:09.950
The first is focused on
people and infrastructure.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:14.100
And again, going back to
one of my main themes,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:15.580
tape is a manual process.

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:16.080
Right?

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:22.370
Tape requires you to manage,
to physically move stuff,

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:29.350
to grab stuff, to move it around
and that takes people to do it.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:33.294
And when you're looking
at the real cost of tape,

00:13:33.294 --> 00:13:34.710
you need to take
that into account

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:38.610
and you need to understand
how efficient your labor is.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.810
You might be anywhere
from a half a person

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:43.550
or a quarter of a person,
up to three, four, five.

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:47.480
I've never seen actually
one bigger than five or six.

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:50.980
But there's also a significant
cost to restore data.

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:53.280
So now the question I
have in italics here is,

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.220
how efficient is your team?

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.640
And you need to factor
that in because it

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:58.940
varies so significantly.

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:01.270
And this is a very
significant piece

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:07.290
of the drivers of your ongoing
operational cost of tape.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:08.840
There's also ongoing
costs associated

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:12.280
with your hardware,
your tape infrastructure

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:19.720
so the hardware maintenance
is anywhere from 19% to 25%.

00:14:19.720 --> 00:14:21.720
You also need to
be concerned with

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:26.840
or think about tape
refresh maintenance.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:29.542
Now you need to think about the
durability of these tapes, when

00:14:29.542 --> 00:14:30.750
you're going to replace them.

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:32.810
Certain tapes are going to fail.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.210
Also, you need to think
about the compatibility

00:14:35.210 --> 00:14:41.490
as formats change as you move
from LTO-4 to 5 to 6 to 7 to 8.

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:46.620
There are some real costs
associated with that.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:49.455
Off-site storage
and transportation.

00:14:49.455 --> 00:14:51.830
Whether you pay for a service
like Iron Mountain to store

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:54.580
your tapes in their
own protective vault

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:59.250
or if you have stored in your
own secure environmentally

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.900
safe vault yourself,
these costs are, in fact,

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:05.240
real and never ending.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.960
And then finally, and this is
one of the really big themes

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:12.160
I mentioned at the
top of this, is

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:16.240
you need to think about
building a redundant or durable

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:17.239
infrastructure.

00:15:17.239 --> 00:15:18.780
This is a really
big one that's often

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:23.680
overlooked when looking at the
economics of tape versus cloud.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.800
If high durability
is important to you,

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:28.250
you'll need to engineer that.

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:31.530
You need to engineer a
certain level of redundancy

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:33.450
to get that durability.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:36.880
It might require two copies or
even three copies of your data.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:40.370
So for cloud, 11 nines of
durability comes built in.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:44.100
So for tape, this could double
or triple the calculation

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:48.074
to have a similar type
level of redundancy.

00:15:48.074 --> 00:15:49.740
A real world example,
I called out here.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:55.390
I had a customer who had north
of 10 petabytes of storage

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:58.080
that were being
put off onto tape.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:02.060
They had three job
redundant copies.

00:16:02.060 --> 00:16:04.340
They refreshed entire
corpus every five years

00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:06.610
and they really provided
a white glove service

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:09.460
that did lots of testing.

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:11.780
This is very important
data to them.

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:14.070
And they calculated,
they paid about $0.04

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:17.300
per gigabyte per
month to maintain

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:19.340
that level of durability.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:22.050
So it's a really
important calculation

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:23.710
as you think through.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:28.995
It's the level of redundancy or
really a level of durability,

00:16:28.995 --> 00:16:30.120
target durability you have.

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:31.150
How many nines?

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:34.250
The question I put here
is, what would it cost you

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:36.280
to delivery 11 nine durability?

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:39.930
How many copies would
you have to have?

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:41.470
Operational cost of tape.

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:44.230
This is the second one
I mentioned before.

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:45.010
Optimisations.

00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:50.850
This is a really often
ignored, often hidden.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:53.010
They're misunderstood costs.

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.480
First in any calculation,
you should take into account

00:16:56.480 --> 00:16:59.390
the actual tape utilization.

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:02.380
It's not typically 100%.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:04.910
Second, you realize that
there's a tremendous amount

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:10.970
of copied or redundant
data that needs to be

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:13.970
or that can be removed.

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:16.660
To call this redundant
data requires work.

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:20.690
And with data locked away on a
tape, requires a lot of work.

00:17:20.690 --> 00:17:24.150
So much so that most people
don't put much of an effort

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:26.280
into cleaning up copies.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.920
A third and related
optimization that's really hard

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.339
is just removing
the garbage data.

00:17:32.339 --> 00:17:33.880
If you look at some
of the stats that

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:36.880
are on the slide, the
redundant data, the IDC claims,

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:40.730
there's 23 generally in
Enterprise or Global 2000

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:41.440
companies.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:44.890
There's on average 23
copies of the same file

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:47.710
and look at all the different
places that a file might live,

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:50.510
all the different backup
copies, all the archive copies,

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:52.339
all the different file servers.

00:17:52.339 --> 00:17:53.880
And it's a challenge
to remove those.

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:55.140
Same with garbage data.

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:57.700
Check Target came out
with the statistic

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:03.470
that a given 30% of an
average organization's data

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:04.550
could be deleted.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:08.310
And so really the question
there is how much effort does

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:12.550
it require to remove this data?

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:19.460
What's required effort
to optimize that data?

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:19.960
All right.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:27.580
So with that, let's move on
to really a short discussion

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:30.070
of networking.

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:33.410
I think networking
is a key thing.

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:36.555
It's frankly just a common
question that I get asked.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.370
Networking, generally
the question

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:45.390
is something along
the lines of, hey,

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:50.260
my network is unreliable,
slow, and therefore,

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:51.344
is the cloud right for me?

00:18:51.344 --> 00:18:53.051
A few things I'm going
to point out here.

00:18:53.051 --> 00:18:55.770
First, backup and archives
typically an offline process.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:59.280
So if we get interrupted,
they can continue

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:00.990
or that's a second piece.

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:04.620
Is they get interrupted,
they can be resumed.

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:07.710
And that's typically built
in to all kinds of software,

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:10.120
including to Google.

00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:11.920
Network throttling's
a common feature

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:18.380
to make sure you don't
consume your network.

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:22.250
Local caching or to a
series of disk arrays,

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:24.570
JBOD is effective and cheap.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:27.350
And DR in the cloud mitigates
many of these concerns.

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:31.780
So if you are going to
recover to the cloud,

00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:34.590
then that's a
really important way

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:38.242
to avoid having to move
of data back on track.

00:19:38.242 --> 00:19:39.700
And the last thing
I'll say on this

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:43.840
is your network can
really be a problem,

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:46.550
but networking speeds,
access reliability,

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:47.890
they're improving all the time.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:49.765
And so it might not be
the right time for you

00:19:49.765 --> 00:19:53.740
now to move to the cloud,
but keep an eye on it

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:56.650
because this is something
that's important to Google

00:19:56.650 --> 00:19:59.890
and it's important to a lot
of government institutions

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:01.830
and lot of government entities.

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:04.430
Speeds are improving.

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:05.870
OK.

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:11.760
So with that, I wanted to
move to a real calculation

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:14.980
or example calculation.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:19.900
So let me go ahead and move
over to an online calculator

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:21.540
that we created.

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:24.700
And this is really just a back
of the envelope calculator.

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.110
The things in yellow
are the things

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:29.460
that can, in fact,
are cells that

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.040
can be changed based
upon the assumptions

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:33.950
that we just talked about.

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:36.320
And it will
calculate out for you

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:42.290
here the annual cost of cloud
and the annual cost of tape.

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:46.100
And what I did was I
determined the annual cost

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:48.740
based upon multiple years.

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:52.000
So I made the assumption
over here the number of years

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:55.030
on tape, you've got the
amount of data up here,

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:56.720
you've got to minimum
number of copies.

00:20:56.720 --> 00:20:59.170
Again, going back to the
what's the level of durability

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:01.820
you expect and how many copies
likely are going to need?

00:21:01.820 --> 00:21:03.550
All in copies.

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:05.420
Then over here,
the key assumptions

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:08.120
are the cost of
your tape library.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:11.790
I took the cost of a
Dell ML6010 library.

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:15.910
The capitalization period
of it I uses as three years.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.330
The cost of IT staff
you'll find as you look

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:20.100
at this, a significant driver.

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:23.340
So I started with a
$90,000 number and then

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:26.930
what's the percentage of time
spent on managing that tape

00:21:26.930 --> 00:21:28.070
infrastructure?

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:30.780
And number of
restores per month.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.660
And then this last one
is really interesting.

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:36.590
It's what likely
optimization can you

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:40.280
get of that 500
terabytes or of that data

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:42.680
that you have up
here in cell B3?

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:44.430
What percentage of
that do you think

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:47.130
you can optimize if you
had easy access to it?

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:47.630
Right?

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:52.310
So this is the, if I can
go back and find those 23

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:55.440
copies that IDC says an
average enterprise has

00:21:55.440 --> 00:22:01.290
or if I can go and find the
30% of data that's just junk

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:02.710
and it can be deleted.

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:04.940
What kind of
optimization can you get?

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:07.390
Because doing it in
tapes not impossible.

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:10.110
It really isn't, but
it's really hard.

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:14.887
It's just almost-- when you
talk to folks who do this,

00:22:14.887 --> 00:22:16.970
it's just almost not worth
putting a lot of effort

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:21.230
into finding all that duplicate
data and all that garbage data.

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:24.490
So as I go through the
assumptions over here

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:26.440
on the right hand
side, in cost of cloud,

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:29.000
we've got the capacity, the
operations, the networking,

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:32.210
and the reads and as
you work through this--

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:35.560
this will be online
in a prettier format

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:37.260
after the webinar--
but today if you

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:42.700
change let's say your
cost of IT staff,

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:46.090
full loaded IT staff is
70,000-- see how it changes.

00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:50.175
Right now, it's $67,000 a year
to protect that 500 terabytes.

00:22:50.175 --> 00:22:51.890
If you go up you
say, you know what?

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:56.530
I can withstand lower-- I have
a lower durability threshold.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:59.850
Maybe I only need one copy/
that brings down your cost.

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:03.810
Let's say the cost of my
tape libraries only 25,000.

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:05.680
You can see how that
changes the cost.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:10.940
Let's say somebody actually
uses 70% or even let's say 100%.

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:13.660
One full person
dedicated to doing this.

00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:18.960
You could see how your average
changes or your cost per year

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:20.610
change.

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:22.190
And of course, you
can go back here

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:26.720
and change the amount of data.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:31.670
Say it's a full petabyte
and we need two copies.

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:33.360
So you can see the
difference here.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:36.190
It's important to figure
out-- and the optimization's,

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:37.165
again, it's at 30%.

00:23:37.165 --> 00:23:39.340
I think this is one of the
really key assumptions.

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:42.800
If you can get 70%, it's going
to dramatically essentially

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:45.700
lower the cost of
cloud because you've

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:48.960
removed a big part of
your petabyte of corpus

00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:51.944
that you needed
to store on tape.

00:23:51.944 --> 00:23:53.360
And if you think
that you're going

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:54.818
to get a very small
percentage, you

00:23:54.818 --> 00:23:58.310
can see how much that increases
the cost to keeping it

00:23:58.310 --> 00:23:59.470
in cloud.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:02.190
And you can see down here
the cost assumptions I used.

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:04.705
The price for
LTO-6 versus LTO-6.

00:24:04.705 --> 00:24:06.080
And this is, again,
a tool that's

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:09.360
going to be made available
online after this webinar.

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:16.980
So let's go ahead and go back
and wrap up this conversation

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:20.279
with some future
considerations and next steps.

00:24:20.279 --> 00:24:22.070
We've talked a lot
about how we [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:25.370
talked about
[INAUDIBLE] and cloud

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:27.055
and what they were intended for.

00:24:27.055 --> 00:24:28.430
We went in and
tried to, I think,

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:32.592
look at some real world examples
of costs associated with each.

00:24:32.592 --> 00:24:34.050
But I did want to
talk a little bit

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:40.910
about the future of a sort
of changing expectations

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:42.530
and changing technologies.

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:47.400
So cloud offers declining
cost on an ongoing basis.

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:48.890
You'll see over
a period of time,

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:50.910
and I probably
should've included

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:54.780
this in this conversation, but
there's declining cost in cloud

00:24:54.780 --> 00:25:01.450
as soon as the cost to us
or to the industry hits.

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:04.430
Typically, they get passed on
some relatively quick period

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:07.220
of time to the
end users, to you,

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:08.765
to the consumer of
the cloud, which

00:25:08.765 --> 00:25:11.940
is to say very different
in a capital outlay.

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:14.600
Not very many
hardware manufacturers

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.950
go back to you six months
after their cost had come down

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:18.890
and say, hey, here's
your money back

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:21.570
because you've, at
that point, obviously

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.320
you've purchased that hardware.

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:27.220
So you have declining cost and
you have increasing utility.

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:30.510
And what we haven't talked about
was the increase in utility.

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:31.960
And what do I mean by that?

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:35.261
It's basically just do more
with your data more easily.

00:25:35.261 --> 00:25:35.760
Right?

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:38.740
So you have lots of
data, it gets put off

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:43.040
on these tape drives, and the
potential value of this data

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:45.620
is locked away.

00:25:45.620 --> 00:25:46.910
It's really hard to get to.

00:25:46.910 --> 00:25:50.550
So if you had that in the
cloud, if we had that online,

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.280
you could do things
like analytics.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:56.800
We have got a product that does
that called BigQuery, BigQ.

00:25:56.800 --> 00:25:59.690
Image analysis, text
and language analysis,

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:04.410
we've got a vision API
and our natural languages

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:06.550
API that are
services that you can

00:26:06.550 --> 00:26:10.740
leverage-- you can lay against
the data that's stored online.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.540
But even more broadly
than that, you

